Sure, I'll provide you with a minimal FastAPI web server example. FastAPI is a modern, fast (high-performance) web framework for building APIs with Python. It is built on top of Starlette for the web parts and Pydantic for the data parts.

Here's a simple FastAPI server:

```python
# main.py

from fastapi import FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/")
async def root():
    return {"message": "Hello World"}

@app.get("/items/{item_id}")
async def read_item(item_id: int):
    return {"item_id": item_id, "name": "Item Name"}

@app.post("/items/")
async def create_item(item: dict):
    return {"item_id": 1, "name": item["name"]}

@app.put("/items/{item_id}")
async def update_item(item_id: int, item: dict):
    return {"item_id": item_id, "name": item["name"]}

@app.delete("/items/{item_id}")
async def delete_item(item_id: int):
    return {"message": f"Item {item_id} deleted"}

if __name__ == "__main__":
    import uvicorn
    uvicorn.run(app, host="127.0.0.1", port=8000)
```

To run this server, you will need to install FastAPI and an ASGI server, such as `uvicorn`. You can install them using pip:

```bash
pip install fastapi uvicorn
```

Then, you can run the server using the following command:

```bash
uvicorn main:app --reload
```

The `--reload` flag enables hot reloading, so the server will automatically restart when you make changes to your code.

You can access the server at `http://127.0.0.1:8000`. The root endpoint will return a JSON response with the message "Hello World". The other endpoints (`/items/{item_id}`, `/items/`, `/items/{item_id}`, and `/items/{item_id}`) demonstrate basic CRUD operations.

