In C++, there isn't a built-in language construct that directly allows you to run the same code inline on multiple named variables. However, you can achieve this by creating a function that takes multiple variables as arguments and then calling that function with different variables. Here's an example of how you can do it:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// A function that takes multiple variables as arguments
void runCode(int &var1, double &var2, char &var3) {
    // Example code to run
    var1 += 1;
    var2 *= 2;
    var3 = 'A';
}

int main() {
    int a = 5;
    double b = 3.5;
    char c = 'Z';

    // Call the function with multiple variables
    runCode(a, b, c);

    // Output the results
    std::cout << "a: " << a << std::endl;
    std::cout << "b: " << b << std::endl;
    std::cout << "c: " << c << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `runCode` function takes three variables (`var1`, `var2`, and `var3`) as references and runs some code on them. You can call this function with different variables in the `main` function to execute the same code on multiple named variables. This approach keeps your code concise and avoids code duplication.

